chore(ci): four short checks in one job, and a real skip for tier 2

A started job bills a whole minute whatever it does, so the two levers
#183 chose both spend more than they look. Four jobs — `go`, `web`,
`secrets`, `workflows` — did well under a minute of work each and
billed four; and a job whose paths did not change billed a minute to
echo that it had nothing to do, which on a push to `main` meant six
jobs and six minutes for a set of commits CI had already proved. Of
the 850 billed minutes measured on 2026-09-19, ~300 of `ci`'s 626 were
this floor (#232).

The four are one job, `checks`. Each area keeps the filter it had, as
a step-level `if:` rather than a job-level one, so what runs is
unchanged and only the packaging moved; the secret scans keep running
on every pull request, since a credential can be added to any file.
The cost is about a minute of wall time, because the four no longer
run in parallel — worth paying on the short checks, which is why
`itest` is not among them.

`itest` instead gets the skip #183 ruled out. Its reasoning was that a
skipped job reports no check and branch protection (#1) needs the
check present; that is true of a workflow skipped by `paths:`, whose
check stays pending forever, and not of a job skipped by an `if:`,
which reports a conclusion of `skipped` that counts as passed. So the
~8-minute job now costs nothing when nothing it covers changed,
instead of a minute to say so — including on every push to `main`,
where it was already filtered out.

The check names change with the jobs: `go`, `web`, `secrets` and
`workflows` become `checks`, which branch protection has to be
configured against when #1 turns it on — and `changes` has to be
required with it. `secrets` was the one job with no `needs:`, so a
failure of the filter job could not silence the secret scan; now
everything is downstream of `changes`, and a skipped job counts as
passed. Two things restore that property: `checks` runs even when the
filter failed, so the scans still run, and it then fails rather than
reading an empty filter and concluding each area had nothing to do.

Part of #232

 — Iris · Frontend Engineer

